# rm189, the one-stroke vintner (Docs/Design/84). Exported from ridgemere3 at revision 1: edit it in Atlas or the DM, or as a draft through tools/mantle_load.py.
mantle rm189
revision 1
state ready
dialect plain
accent light broad
tier full
written_for rm_delphine_saltwick
written_by codex
title the one-stroke vintner
fits stage=adult|old
prefers role=merchant job=merchant temper=short
traits temper=short talk=plain warmth=reserved humour=none honesty=frank piety=ordinary drive=steady
quirk cuts_bottle_wax_with_one_stroke_of_a_pocket_blade
likes spiced_cider dancing a_full_till misty_dawns
dislikes haggling night_noise
fears a_cask_gone_to_vinegar a_short_till_at_the_quarter
hopes one_more_season_of_reels a_full_till_every_marketday
topics work +0.5 prices +0.4 festival +0.3 pastimes +0.3 weather +0.2 food +0.2 caravan +0.2 gossip -0.3 faith -0.2
styles comfort=practical gossip=reluctant refuse=short tease=none
marks oath saying
persona #self.first# keeps a counter in the Commons with the price chalked above it and will not haggle, cuts the wax off a bottle neck with one stroke of a pocket blade kept keen on a wet stone, walks out for the misty dawns, and dances hard on a feast night.

words
oath! = Lees and lamplight | Cork and candle-end | Spigot and sawdust
saying! = Good wine doesn't argue | Chalk doesn't bargain | What's chalked is chalked
blade = my blade | the little wax blade
stone = the stone | my wet stone
wax = the wax | the seal wax on the neck
cask = the cask | the old cask under the arch
casks = the casks | the racks of casks
cellar = the cellar | the vaults under the street
slate = the slate | the price slate over the counter
till = the till | a till that shuts heavy
cider = spiced cider | cider with the cloves left in
mist = a misty dawn | the mist coming up off the Sound
reel = a reel | a proper reel with a fiddle in it
haggler = a haggler | some penny-pincher with opinions
noise = the night noise | singing and cartwheels under the window till all hours
vinegar = vinegar | a cask gone sour
corks = the corks | a crate of new corks
rise = the Rise | the gravel drives above the chapel roofs
stall = the counter | my counter
steps = the cellar steps | the sixteen steps under the street

any
opinion @1 city for 1 : I was born on these slopes and I'll be carried down them, and nobody runs the place down at my counter meanwhile. | Wet, steep and dear, and mine. I'd not swap it.
opinion @1 merchants for 1 : Settle one carter's bill, sweep up a broken bottle and you'll know where a price comes from. I don't grudge an honest seller the difference.
opinion @2 merchants for 2 [world.wagons=late|raided] : Every load that's late, the seller pays for it first. Then the buyer. Then everyone grumbles at the seller.
opinion @1 newcomers for 1 : New folk drink, same as old folk. I'll serve them, and I'll watch the count while I do.
opinion @1 road against 1 : Half of what I sell came up a road, and the road takes longer every season. I chalk the difference and I'm called greedy for it.
opinion @1 strangers for 1 : A traveller leaning on my counter buys a bottle and tells me what's on the road. That's a fair bargain.
opinion @1 watch for 1 : The Watch pass my door at midnight and again at three, and nobody's lifted a bottle off my counter in years. I'll say good morning to them for that.
opinion @1 festivals mixed 1 : I'll dance the fiddler into the ground, and then I'd like the city to go home quietly. It never does.
opinion @1 drink mixed 1 : A glass with supper is a kindness. A bottle before noon is a fool's errand, and I've sold to both.
opinion @1 houses mixed 0 : The Rise buys the best and pays the slowest. They get served like anyone, and their reckoning goes up twice.
opinion @1 dole for 1 : An empty belly in a town this rich shames every one of us. My coin goes in the chapel box, and the sum's my own affair.
opinion @1 times against 1 [world.times=hard|lean] : When times pinch, wine's dropped from the basket before bread is, and missed long after.

grown
line @1 greet : #greet#. Watch the doorstep, it dips in the middle. | #greet#. Plain speaking's welcome at this door.
line @2 greet [pair.band=strangers] : #greet#. New to this street, by the look. The counter's this way. | #greet#. Your face is a stranger to me yet. A few visits will cure that.
line @3 greet [self.working=yes self.role=merchant] : #greet#. One moment, I've #wax# to cut. | #greet#. I'm at the counter, so talk while I work.
line @4 greet [here.time=dawn] : Morning. Was it misty your way? It was thick on mine. | Morning. You're out early. So am I. I like it.
line @5 greet [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. My feet have finished, even if I haven't. | Evening. A late caller. Well, so be it.
line @6 greet [pair.band=friends|family] : Ah. I'd begun to think the hill had eaten you. | Good. I've been short of sensible company all day.
line @1 greet.return : #greet#. | #greet#. Likewise, I'm sure.
line @2 greet.return [pair.band=friends|family] : And you. Get out of the wet. | Hello yourself. You look well.
line @3 greet.return [self.working=yes] : #greet#. Mind your elbows near #casks#. | #greet#. You'll forgive me working.
line @4 greet.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Evening. Don't keep me long. | Evening to you.
line @1 kin.open [pair.kin!=no] : Sit down. I'll not have you standing about. | You've eaten, I hope. Don't lie to me.
line @1 again.open : Twice today. My doorstep will wear thin. | Here again. The last chat's still warm.
line @1 hail [pair.band=friends] : Over here. I'm not shouting twice. | Hoy. Spare me a minute, you.
line @1 farewell : #farewell#. Mind the step going out. | #farewell#. Bring me a thirst or a decent reason for the visit.
line @2 farewell : #saying.capitalize#. Off you go. | That's me done. #farewell#.
line @3 farewell [here.time=dusk|night] : Go home before the singers start. #farewell#. | Get indoors. The night's no friend to anyone.
line @1 farewell.return : And you. Don't dawdle in the wet. | #farewell#. Counter and I both stay put.
line @2 farewell.return [here.time=dusk|night] : Night, then. Lock your door. | Sleep, if they let you.
line @1 react.news : Go on. And keep it short. | Tell it straight. Not the dressed-up version.
line @1 react.news.new : Nobody's carried that in yet. Somebody will. | First I've heard. You're sure of it?
line @1 react.news.known : I've had that three times today across the counter. | Old news by noon. Somebody always comes in with it.
line @1 react.news.doubt : Somebody who saw it will be in for a bottle soon. I'll ask them. | That's been through a few mouths. I can taste it.
line @1 react.news.sorrow [topic.kind=death|loss|theft|assault|arrest|crime] : Hard, that. It sits ill with me. | That's an ill thing. I'll send round a bottle, and no talk of paying.
line @1 react.news.glad [topic.kind=marriage|birth|apprentice|succession|work] : Good. That wants a glass raising. | Good news, and I'll be telling it over the counter all week.
line @1 answer.how : Well enough. My knees argue, I don't listen. | Steady. The till's honest and so am I.
line @1 answer.how.busy [self.working=yes] : Busy. Don't stand where the casks roll. | Up before the lamplighter finished, standing till he's back.
line @1 ask.how : You're keeping well? You look it. | How's the world with you? Honestly, now.
line @1 sympathise : Heavy, that. Lean on my counter a minute. | I'm sorry. Few words from me, but each one meant.
line @1 reassure : Give it a dry week. Most things mend in one. | You'll manage. You have that sort of face.
line @1 decline : I'll not. It's another's to hand over, not mine, so ask them. | Not today. The counter's mine alone and my paws are full.
line @2 decline [self.role=merchant] : What's on the slate stays on the slate. Not for you, not for the Rise. | I'll not knock it down. I'd rather lose the sale than start that.
line @1 accept_decline : Fair. Your business, not mine. | Then that's an end of it. No harm done.
line @1 thank : Kindly done. It's remembered, not entered. | Thank you. I'm sparing with those, so keep it.
line @1 deflect_thanks : It was nothing worth thanks. | Don't. It's done and dusted.
line @1 preclose [self.working=yes] : I've #wax# to cut and #corks# to count. | That's enough standing about for me. The counter wants me.
line @2 preclose : Well. That's the last inch of your morning I'll take. | Go on, your day's waiting.
line @1 accept_preclose : Off with you. Those racks could use my attention. | Right. Away with you.
line @1 take_offence : I heard that. Once was plenty. | Mind your tongue in my doorway.
line @1 apologise : Sharp of me. I'm sharp with the whole street; don't take it home. | That came out sharper than I meant. I'm sorry for it.
line @1 insist : No. Said, and meant, and that's me. | Once more, then, and then I've done with it.
line @1 ack : Right. | Mm. | So.
line @1 give.advice : Buy the second-best and drink it sooner. That's all the secret there is. | Never store a bottle standing up, and never trust a cork that squeaks.
line @1 warn [world.bandits=near] : After dark, keep within the walls. I've had carters at my counter shaking. | Past the gate, take company or don't go.
line @1 congratulate : Good. You've worked for that, plain to see. | Well done. I'll pour you something for it.
scene sc_one_stroke
when topic=work|smalltalk self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : One stroke. That's how you take #wax# off a neck. Two and you've chipped the glass. | Watch the wrist, not the blade. The wax comes off in one curl. | Folk ask for the curls. Why, I couldn't say.
b : One stroke? Every time? | It's like peeling an apple. | What do they do with them?
a : Every time since I learned. I missed once in a fog, and it nags me yet. | Better. An apple doesn't fight back. Old wax does. | Children keep them. One old fellow burns them for luck.
b : That's a good hand. | #bc_ha# | Takes all sorts.
a : It's a practised one. That's all a good hand is. | There's a curl on my step most days for the taking. | It does. I just sell the wine.

scene sc_wet_stone
when topic=smalltalk|work
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / backchannel / assess
a : Before I unlock each day, #blade# gets twenty passes on #stone#. | I wet #stone# from the rain barrel. Rain's free and it's never short here. | A dull blade's a danger. It slips, and then you've a cut paw and wine on the floor.
b : Why twenty? | Does the rain water matter? | Have you cut yourself?
a : Nineteen wasn't enough and twenty-one was showing off. | Not a bit. It's just nearest. | Once, years ago. Never since. That's what the stone's for.
b? : #bc_wise# | #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_lucky#
a : I don't count out loud, mind. Not any more. | There we are. Nothing mysterious about it. | It's not luck. It's twenty strokes.

scene sc_no_haggling
when topic=prices|work self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / insist / backchannel / assess
a : A fellow tried to haggle me this morning. Over one bottle. | I chalk every price up to spare folk asking. They ask anyway. | #haggler.capitalize# came in yesterday and tried three different prices on me.
b : Did you give way? | Some folk enjoy it. | Three?
a : I lifted the bottle back off the counter and racked it. He bought it the next day at the price. | Then they'll find their fun two doors down. I've no time for the dance. | Three, each one meaner than the last. I said no three times.
b : Stubborn. | #bc_ha# | #bc_fair_enough#
a : Steady, I call it. #saying#. | #saying#. Done. | And the price stayed where it was. It always does.

scene sc_haggle_trade
when topic=prices self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does give.opinion / react.self / counter / backchannel / assess
a : I don't haggle. Folk think it's pride. It's arithmetic. | Haggling's for folk with idle afternoons. I have a counter. | If I knock off for one, I knock off for all of them. Word travels in this street.
b[role=merchant] #trade : I haggle. It brings them back. | I build the haggle into the price. | It does. Fast.
b : That's hard on a poor buyer. | Most sellers do it. | So you never give?
a@trade : It brings them back to haggle again. I'd rather they came back to buy. | Then your price is a fib in a clean apron. Mine is just a price. | Faster than the carts. So I don't start.
a : I'll set aside the small bottle for a poor buyer. I'll not set aside the price. | Most sellers do as they please. I chalk my price, and I stand by it. | I give good wine, full measure and a straight answer. That's plenty.
b : #bc_true_enough# | Fair. | #bc_right#
a : So. Nobody leaves cheated, and nobody leaves smug. | That's my way, and I sleep well. | Good. Then we know where we stand.

scene sc_full_till
when topic=work self.role=merchant here.time=dusk|night
does tell.feeling / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : On a good day #till# shuts with a thump like a cellar door. | I count the takings twice at dusk. Once to know, once to be sure. | Marketday's last count with the drawer near bursting. Keep your palaces.
b : It must be a good feeling. | And if they don't match? | Not even a little palace?
a : The best I know that doesn't need a fiddle. | They match. I count slowly the first time. | Palaces leak. I've seen the Rise in a storm.
b : #bc_ha# | Good. | #bc_true#
a : Then it's locked, and I'm off up the stairs. | Then I sleep. Simple as that. | So. The till for me.

scene sc_slow_counter
when topic=work|times self.role=merchant self.business=failing
does tell.worry / reassure / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Quiet at the counter all week. I've dusted the same row twice. | Nobody's buying the good bottles. They're buying nothing at all. | I've had more folk in to shelter from the rain than to buy.
b : It'll pick up. | Everyone's watching their coin. | At least they came in.
a : It had better. Wine doesn't wait for good times; it waits for vinegar. | I know it. I'm watching mine too. | They did. I'd have sold them a cup if any of them had asked.
b : #reassure# | #bc_poor_soul# | Next time, perhaps.
a : I'll keep the door unbarred. A counter can't do more. | We'll see. I've had lean years before. | Perhaps. I'll leave the cups on the counter.

scene sc_busy_counter
when topic=work self.role=merchant self.business=thriving
does tell.boast / congratulate / deflect_praise / backchannel / assess
a : I've sold the last of the dark red and there's a list for the next lot. | Three crates out the door before noon. My sales book never used this much ink. | They're queuing on the steps. In the rain. For wine.
b : Well done, you. | You'll be rich. | That's a good sign.
a : It's the wine, not me. I just keep it cool. | Rich, no. Comfortable, and that does me. | Aye, but I don't trust it. Good weeks end.
b : #bc_good# | Comfortable's good. | #bc_wise#
a : Still. I'll take it. | So. I'm not complaining. | Till then, I'll keep pouring.

scene sc_misty_dawn
when topic=weather here.time=dawn here.weather=fog
does tell.feeling / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : #mist.capitalize#. I took the chapel way round purely to walk through it. | The end of the street's gone this morning. Suits me. | Fog like this, every street hushes. Even the carts creep.
b : It's damp, though. | It's eerie. | Quieter's better.
a : Everything here's damp. At least this damp is pretty. | Not to me. For one hour the city has manners. | It is. I'll have it gone by noon, but I'll have had it.
b : #bc_lovely# | #bc_ha# | So you will.
a : My hour, before anyone wants a thing from me. | I'd walk it every day if it came every day. | Then back to the counter and its noise.

scene sc_mist_cellar
when topic=weather|work here.time=dawn self.role=merchant
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / backchannel / assess
a : I open #cellar# at dawn and let the cool in. You can smell the casks breathing. | I go down #steps# while the street's still snoring. My hour. | First light, I'm down below with a lamp, checking for drips.
b : Breathing? | Why so early? | And are there drips?
a : Wood breathes. You'll smell it on a wet morning, sharp and sweet. | Because nobody comes down asking prices. | One, under the arch. Same one for six years. I've a bucket for it.
b : #bc_lovely# | #bc_fair# | #bc_ha#
a : Then up, unbar, and the questions start. | An hour's quiet is worth an hour's sleep. | It's the one leak I trust.

scene sc_rain_steps
when topic=weather here.weather=rain|storm here.region=ridgemere
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : The rain's coming down #steps# like a stream. I've put sacks across the bottom. | Water in the cellar is the end of good labels. I've moved the best bottles up. | Rain again. The street runs downhill and takes half the mud with it.
b : #grumble# | Will it get in? | It always does, here.
a : Sacks and a broom. I've nothing grander. | Not past the sacks. I'll be down twice tonight to check. | Always. You'd think somebody up the hill would build a drain.
b : #bc_good# | Good luck. | #bc_ha#
a : It'll hold. It usually holds. | #oath#. I hate a wet label. | They won't. They live up the hill.

scene sc_cider_weather
when topic=season|weather here.season=winter|autumn
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Cold enough for #cider# now. I've had the pot going since the first frost. | There's #cider# warming by the counter in this weather. Not for sale. | Once the wind turns, I start on the cloves. Cloves reach three doors along.
b : Not for sale? | It does smell good. | First frost already?
a : Not for sale. For whoever's honest with me today. | Cloves, orange peel and a stick of cinnamon. That's all I'll say. | Last night. My pads knew before my eyes did.
b : #bc_ha# | More than most get. | It's early this year.
a : Most days the pot goes home full. | It's more than I tell most. Don't spread it. | Early or late, the pot goes on.

scene sc_cider_recipe
when topic=food
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / backchannel / assess
a : I make my own #cider#. Have done since I was small. | Folk ask me for the recipe every winter. They don't get it. | There's one thing in my cider nobody guesses. I've had years of guesses.
b : Since you were small? | Why not share it? | What's the thing?
a : Since I could reach the pot without a stool. | Because then it's everyone's, and it's not mine any more. | That secret's mine and the pot's.
b : #bc_lovely# | Fair enough. | #bc_ha#
a : And it's better now than it was then, I'll say that. | A cup, you may have. The pot's not for sharing. | Keep guessing. Everyone does.

scene sc_supper_glass
when topic=food here.time=dusk|night
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : A cup of something red with supper. Not two. One. | I take bread and cheese and a small glass, and I'm in bed before the noise starts. | Supper's plain in my house. The wine's the only grand thing on the table.
b : Just one? | Wise. | Grand enough.
a : One's a pleasure. Two's a habit. I've seen both across the counter. | Wise, or old. I'll take either. | It's enough. Good bread doesn't need help.
b : #bc_true# | #bc_ha# | #bc_true_enough#
a : So, one. And the cork back in. | That's my evening. Don't laugh. | So. Simple suppers.

scene sc_dancing
when topic=pastimes|smalltalk
does confide / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : I dance. Folk are always surprised. | Let a fiddle start and my heels forget their age by the second bar. | My knees complain all week and then a reel starts and they forget.
b : You? Dance? | Every time? | They forget?
a : Me. From my first feast on, and nobody's managed to sit me down yet. | Every time. I can't sit still for one. Never could. | Completely. Then they remember, the morning after.
b : Now there's a sight I'd pay for. | #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#
a : Come to the next feast. I'll be the one the fiddler's frightened of. | So, mind your feet if you're near me. | That's the cost. I pay it gladly.

scene sc_feast_reel
when topic=festival|pastimes here.day=festival
does tell.feeling / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Feast night. The counter's shut and I'm going dancing. | I've polished my good shoes for tonight. Don't tell anyone. | There'll be fiddling on Market Square by dusk, and I'll be under it.
b : You'll dance all night? | Your good shoes? | You love it.
a : Till the fiddler goes home or I do, and it'll not be me first. | The black ones, with the buckles. Older than half the fiddlers and still dancing. | I do. Nothing else gets me so little cross.
b : #bc_good# | Lovely. | #bc_ha#
a : So, I'll see you there or I won't, but you'll hear me. | They'll last me out. They were made properly. | Go and see for yourself. I'll be the loud one's partner.

scene sc_after_feast
when topic=festival here.time=night
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : I'll dance as long as anyone, but I'd like the city to go quiet after. It won't. | There's a fellow below my shutters bawling a song with no words left in it. | The feast was lovely. The four hours of noise after were not.
b : Feasts don't come often. | #bc_ha# | You'll not sleep.
a : Now and again's plenty when you open at dawn. | I could go down and teach him the words. | I'll not. And I'll be at the counter at dawn regardless.
b : #bc_true_enough# | Go on, then. | #bc_poor_soul#
a : So. Earplugs of wool and a pillow. | I won't. They'd only sing louder. | There it is. Feast one day, short temper the next.

scene sc_night_noise
when topic=home here.time=night
does tell.complaint / react.complaint / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : #noise.capitalize#. I'm up at dawn and the street's up till midnight. | A cart went over the cobbles at midnight and woke me. Then another. | It's never the rain keeps me awake. It's whoever's out in it, shouting.
b : #grumble# | That's the city. | Shouting what?
a : I keep a decent hour. I'd like the street to keep one too. | Oh, it's the city. I'd love it more with the wheels oiled. | Nothing worth hearing. Songs, mostly. Badly.
b : #bc_i_know# | #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#
a : I've said it to the Watch. They nodded. That was all. | So. Wool in the ears and on with it. | Cross by noon, I'll be, and rightly.

scene sc_rise_customers
when topic=lore|smalltalk here.region=ridgemere self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : The Rise sends a servant down for its wine and pays at the quarter, if I'm lucky. | #rise.capitalize# buys the best I've got and never asks the price. | One house up the hill sends a boy with a list in a better hand than mine.
b : At least they pay. | Never asks? | Fancy.
a : They pay. Slowly. I send the reckoning twice and smile both times. | Never. I'd rather they asked. It's a kind of manners. | Fancy paper and plain sums. Same wine as everyone gets.
b : #bc_ha# | Odd. | #bc_ha#
a : So. Hilltop coin rings the same as anyone's. | That's the hilltop all over. Polite about everything but money. | And they get the same answer: what's chalked.

scene sc_sump_below
when topic=smalltalk|times here.region=ridgemere
does tell.worry / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : I look down the hill at the Sump some mornings and I'm glad of my own roof. | There's folk below the market can't afford bread, let alone a bottle. I don't forget it. | I send the broken bottles' wine down to the chapel kitchen. Waste not.
b : It's hard down there. | You're lucky. | That's kind of you.
a : Hard, aye. A bottle mends none of it; I'm not daft. | I'm lucky and I worked. Both. Down there they work and aren't lucky. | It's not kindness. It's not pouring good wine in the gutter.
b : #bc_true# | #bc_true_enough# | #bc_fair_enough#
a : So. Some goes down the hill, and I don't crow. | So. My view, for what it weighs. | Name it how you please. It's put to use.

scene sc_carts_late
when topic=caravan|prices world.wagons=late self.role=merchant
does tell.complaint / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : No carts yet, and I've a gap on the shelf where the white should be. | Each day without carts, I'm chalking a higher price and being glared at. | Late. Again. I could set the calendar by how late they are.
b : They'll come. | It's not your fault. | Again?
a : They'll come, and then everyone wants everything at once. | Tell that to the folk glaring. | Again. I've stopped expecting and started hoping.
b : #bc_i_hope_so# | #bc_true# | #bc_hope_so#
a : I'll be ready when they do. That's all I can be. | So. I chalk it and I bear it. | Hoping's cheaper. Barely.

scene sc_carts_in
when topic=caravan world.wagons=in self.role=merchant
does tell.news / react.news.glad / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Wagons arrived. Crates below, and an aching back up top. | Stock's in. I can fill the gap on the shelf at last. | Wagons came up the road this morning. Good, and about time.
b : That's a relief. | You'll sleep tonight. | #bc_about_time#
a : It is. I'll not chalk the price up, either, which is a relief to everyone. | I'll sleep after I've checked every bottle for cracks. | That's what I said to the carter. He didn't laugh.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#
a : So. Back to ordinary. | Then sleep. | So. Back to work.

scene sc_carts_raided
when topic=caravan|bandits world.wagons=raided
does tell.worry / sympathise / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : A cart fell to robbers beyond the toll road. Whose, I don't yet know. | A load robbed outside the walls. Somebody's lost a season on that. | Robbers again. My crates won't come, and crates are nothing beside it.
b : #pity# | That's cruel. | I hope nobody was hurt.
a : It is. I'll know more when somebody comes in to buy. They always do. | It is. And somebody's family waiting on that load. | So do I. Crates can be bought again.
b : #bc_i_hope_so# | #bc_true# | #bc_true#
a : So. Chalk it and wait. That's all a counter's for. | Whoever it was gets a bottle from me, no charge. | Waiting, then. A counter's good at waiting.

scene sc_lean_times
when topic=times|prices world.times=hard|lean
does tell.worry / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Times like these, folk buy the small bottle or nothing. Mostly nothing. | Short coin sends wine out of the basket first. I don't blame them. | I've had regulars come in, look at the slate, and go out again.
b : You'll get by. | It's the same everywhere. | Did you say anything?
a : I'll get by. I'm worried for the ones who won't. | Everywhere. My cellar's full and my till's empty. | No. I'll not shame anyone for counting.
b : #bc_i_hope_so# | #bc_poor_soul# | Good.
a : So. Good wine keeps. It'll be here when they come back. | There it is. It'll turn. | When they can, they'll come back. I'll be here.

scene sc_good_times
when topic=times|prices world.times=good|boom
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Folk have coin again. The good red's selling faster this month than it did all of last year. | Good times. Even the Sump's buying a bottle for Restday. | Everyone's flush, and everyone suddenly knows about wine.
b : That's good news. | Even the Sump? | Suddenly?
a : I'll salt some away for the lean year. There's always a lean year. | Even there. Small bottles, but bought. That's something. | Suddenly. They'll forget it again when the coin goes.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_good# | #bc_ha#
a : So. Fill the till and keep a bit back. | I'll not complain. Not this month. | So. I'll sell while they're learning.

scene sc_gossip_held
when topic=gossip here.gossip=yes
does tell.gossip / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Across my counter came this: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That's all I know. | Here's what the cider crowd brought me: #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That's my lot. | You'll hear it anyway. #topic.subject.capitalize# #topic.claim#. That's the story going round.
b : Is it true? | And what do you think? | That's news.
a : No idea, and I won't guess over a counter. Guesses get repeated. | My judgement? Ask for a glass and I'll judge what's in it. | It is. And I've told you, so that's my share of it done.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_fair# | #bc_right#
a : So. Not another word from me. | There. Chew on it yourself. | Done. Now buy something or let me work.

scene sc_gossip_none
when topic=gossip here.gossip!=yes
does give.opinion / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Nothing from me, and I'd sit on it if I had any. | Half the street leans on my counter to talk. I let it all go by. | Gossip's for folk with time. I have a till.
b : Not even a little? | You must hear plenty. | #bc_ha#
a : Not even a little. It's not my wine to pour. | Plenty. And I forget it before the door shuts. | That's not a joke. It's my day.
b : #bc_fair_enough# | #bc_wise# | Fair.
a : So. Ask me about the wine instead. | A counter stays honest that way. | So. There it is.

scene sc_newcomer
when topic=newcomer
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : There's a newcomer about, two streets over. Came in, read the slate and paid. I liked that. | There's somebody new moved in near the market. I'll know their tastes in a month. | A newcomer asked for something sweet. I've put them down as a white-wine sort.
b : That's a good start. | A month? | You can tell?
a : Best start there is. No haggling, no fuss. | A month and three bottles. That's how long it takes. | Usually. Folk drink the way they talk.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#
a : They'll do. | We'll see. I'm not often wrong. | So. We'll see what they drink at the feast.

scene sc_neighbour
when topic=friends|home self.neighbour
does tell.feeling / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : I'd hand #self.neighbour# my key to #cellar#, and few get that from me. | For #self.neighbour# I'd cross the road just to nod. That's our friendship. It's a good one. | If I'm laid up, it's #self.neighbour# I'd want fetched.
b : That's a good neighbour. | Just a nod? | You'd need someone.
a : It is. Good neighbours are rarer than good wine. | A nod's enough between sensible folk. | I would. I'd not admit it out loud, mind.
b : #bc_true# | #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#
a : So. That's the street for you, at its best. | There it is. | So. Now you know, and don't repeat it.

scene sc_the_hand
when topic=work self.hand self.role=merchant
does tell.feeling / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : #self.hand# is the only one I let near the good racks. That's trust, from me. | I'd give #self.hand# the till key, and I've given it to nobody else in years. | I'm short with #self.hand#, same as with everyone. I'd be lost without #self.hand#, mind.
b : Have you said so aloud? | That's a lot. | I expect that comes across.
a : No. Heads swell. | Aye. Keep it quiet. | I'd hope so. I'll not be saying it.
b : #bc_ha# | Fair. | #bc_ha#
a : So. Some things are better shown than said. | Good. There it is. | So. That's that.

scene sc_teaching
when topic=work self.master=yes
does tell.self / ask.detail / answer.detail / backchannel / assess
a : If I take a learner, first thing they learn is the cork, not the wine. | I teach a learner to stand a full minute before they taste. Nobody likes it. | I'll teach anybody the trade who can count and keep their temper. I can count.
b : Why the cork first? | Why a full minute? | And the temper?
a : A bad cork spoils a good bottle. You learn to hear it, the squeak and the give. | The wine's been waiting years. It can wait a minute more. So can they. | The temper's the hard part. I've never mastered mine, and I'd be lying to say so.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_ha# | #bc_ha#
a : So. Corks, then wine, then the slate. | That's my teaching. They thank me later. | So. Half the trade's better than none.

scene sc_old_street
when topic=memories self.age>=50 here.region=ridgemere
does recall / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : When I was small this street had a well at the top and three goats. | I remember when the market had no roof and we sold out of the rain on a good day. | There was a cooper on this corner when I was small. You'd hear the hammer from the chapel.
b : Goats? Here? | No roof at all? | Gone now?
a : Goats. They ate a lady's hat. I've not forgotten it. | None. We stood under sacking and called it a stall. | Gone. The hammer's gone with him. I still listen for it, mornings.
b : #bc_ha# | Hard days. | #bc_poor_soul#
a : So. The street's older, and I'm older, and both of us creak. | Hard and wet. I'd not go back. | So. Things change. Not always worse.

scene sc_chapel
when topic=faith
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Restdays I'm on the back bench at chapel, mouth shut while they sing. | A candle from me twice a year. Not for show. | Prayers aren't my strength. A full till, though, earns a quiet word upward to the Bright One.
b : Why the back? | For whom? | That's a prayer of sorts.
a : So I'm out the door first and down to the counter. | That's mine to know. | That one I can mean.
b : #bc_ha# | #bc_fair# | #bc_ha#
a : The Bright One understands. I'd hope so. | So. Twice a year. | So. A plain prayer for a plain wolf.

scene sc_knees
when topic=health self.age>=50
does tell.complaint / sympathise / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : My knees forecast better than any almanac. They've been at me since dawn. | The cellar steps get steeper every year. I've counted; there's still sixteen. | I'll not be told to sit down. I'll sit when I'm dead.
b : #sympathy# | Sixteen's a lot. | Well said.
a : I'll live. #remedy.capitalize#, and a lot of not listening to them. | It's always been sixteen. It's me that's changed, more's the pity. | Thank you. Everyone else tells me to rest.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_poor_soul# | #bc_ha#
a : Then up I get, same as ever. | So. Sixteen, and up I go. | So. I'll rest at the feast. Dancing.

scene sc_autumn_casks
when topic=season|harvest here.season=autumn self.role=merchant
does tell.self / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Autumn's when the new stock comes up the road. I clear a rack for it in the first cold week. | This is the season I count everything twice. New casks, old casks, empties. | The leaves come down and the price slate goes up. Every year.
b : A lot of work. | Twice? | Every year?
a : It is. I'll not have it done by anyone else. | Twice. Once in the morning, once after supper, when I'm sharper. | Every year. The road's no shorter in autumn.
b : #bc_good# | #bc_ha# | #bc_true#
a : So. A busy month and a sore back. | That's the trade. | So. I chalk it and bear it.

scene sc_crime_heard
when topic=crime world.crime=recent
does tell.worry / react.self / tell.self / backchannel / assess
a : Trouble about town, from what the counter hears. I've put the bar across the cellar door. | Somebody was robbed, they're saying. I'll not leave the till in the window tonight. | Trouble again, by what comes across the counter. Busy nights for the Watch.
b : Best to be careful. | Wise. | #bc_true#
a : Careful's free. Bottles aren't. | Wise, or just old. Often the same. | They'll pass my door twice. My doorstep gives me a decent view till then.
b : #bc_wise# | #bc_ha# | #bc_good#
a : So. Bar on, lamp out. | So. Till upstairs. | There it is.

story st_first_curl [topic=memories|work self.age>=40 self.role=merchant]
  : My first try at the wax on a bottle took four strokes and a kitchen knife.
  : An old cellarman watched me do it. He took the knife off me, gave me a small blade and told me one stroke, and not till I could.
  ask : How long would you guess it took me?
  b : A season, I'd guess.
  : Two winters. And I've the blade still, on my belt, ground down to half what it was.

story st_buckle_shoes [topic=pastimes|festival self.age>=40]
  : Years back at a feast, my shoe buckle flew off halfway through a reel.
  : I danced the rest of it with one shoe flapping. I'd not stop for a buckle.
  ask : Would you have stopped?
  b : I'd have found a stool for my nerves. Did anyone notice?
  : The whole square. They cheered. I'd have gone red, if I'd stopped long enough to.

story st_haggler_back [topic=work|prices self.age>=30 self.role=merchant]
  : There was a fellow once who argued my price for half an hour and walked out.
  : Seven days later he was back, laid the full price down, and kept his mouth shut.
  ask : Guess what I told him?
  b : Something sharp, knowing you.
  : I thanked him, and handed over the better bottle. He never haggled again.

story st_bells_in_fog [topic=weather|memories self.age>=30 here.region=ridgemere]
  : Mist took my paws clean away one morning. I could feel the sea wall under them and see nothing.
  : Then the chapel bell went, and I could hear the ships answering it out on the Sound.
  b : That sounds strange. Were you frightened?
  : No. It was the most beautiful thing I ever heard. I go out every misty dawn hoping for it again.

story st_vinegar_cask [topic=work|memories self.age>=40 self.role=merchant]
  : Years ago I lost a whole cask to vinegar. A bad seal, and I'd not checked it.
  : I poured it down the gutter myself, every drop, so I'd remember.
  ask : Do you think I've forgotten it?
  b : Not by the way you tell it.
  : I check every seal every morning. That cask taught me more than any master.

rule @bk_slate
when concept=bark self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does aside
cooldown 6h
say Prices are on the slate. Read it before you ask. | Good red, fair white, and no haggling.

rule @bk_wax
when concept=bark self.role=merchant self.working=yes
does aside
cooldown 8h
say One stroke. There. | Where's my stone got to? This blade's going dull.

rule @bk_haggle
when concept=bark self.dislikes=haggling
does aside
cooldown 10h
say If one more wolf offers me half, I'll shut the door on them. | Read the chalk. #saying#.

rule @bk_night
when concept=bark here.time=night
does aside
cooldown 10h
say Is there no end to the singing in this street? | Somebody oil that cart, for pity's sake.

rule @bk_mist
when concept=bark here.time=dawn here.weather=fog
does aside
cooldown 12h
say There it is. #mist.capitalize#. Lovely. | Fog to the rooftops. Good.

rule @bk_carts
when concept=bark world.wagons=late self.role=merchant
does aside
cooldown 12h
say Still no carts. #oath#. | Another day with a gap on the shelf.

dlg @1 resident.hub : #greet#. A straight question gets a straight answer here. | #greet#. You've caught me with a free minute. Use it.
dlg @2 resident.hub [topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. Here again. Today's want? | #greet#, #topic.player#. I'd know you anywhere. Paid without a quibble.
dlg @3 resident.hub [here.time=night] : It's late. Be brief and I'll be plain. | Night. I'm off up to bed shortly. What is it?
dlg @4 resident.hub [pair.disposition=hostile|wary] : I'll talk to you from here, thank you. Say your piece. | Liking you comes later, if it comes. Go on.
dlg @1 resident.job [self.role=merchant] : I keep #casks# and a counter, and I chalk the prices so nobody need ask. | #self.trade_words.capitalize#. Corks and wax and a slate, and I'll not haggle.
dlg @2 resident.job : I work, I count honest, I go home. | Steady work. No grumbling and no crowing.
dlg @1 resident.bye : #farewell#. Mind the step. | #farewell#. Bring a good story next visit.
dlg @2 resident.bye [here.time=night] : Get indoors. #farewell#. | Night. Lock your door.
dlg @1 resident.news [world.news] : Came across the counter from #world.news_from#: #world.news#. I had no more than that. | From #world.news_from#, so they say: #world.news#.
dlg @2 resident.news [world.event_words] : Every mouth in the street says #world.event_words#. Since dawn, nothing else. | #world.event_words.capitalize#. Everyone who buys a bottle tells me again.
dlg @3 resident.news [world.wagons=raided] : Word of a cart stripped out beyond the gates. Bolt up early. | A load went missing past the walls. I'm waiting to hear whose.
dlg @4 resident.news [!world.news !world.event_words world.wagons!=raided world.death!=recent world.wedding!=recent] : Nothing's come across the counter worth repeating. | Quiet. The carts are the news, and they've said nothing.
dlg @1 resident.gossip [here.gossip=yes] : They say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. I've told you, and that's my share done. | The cider crowd say #topic.subject# #topic.claim#. That's the lot.
dlg @2 resident.gossip [here.gossip!=yes] : I don't pour gossip. Ask me about the wine. | Nothing I'd repeat at my own counter.
dlg @1 resident.business [business=thriving] : Better than I've known it. I've a list for the next delivery. | Good. The till shuts heavy every night.
dlg @2 resident.business [business=steady] : Steady. The till's honest and so am I. | Steady enough. No complaints worth making.
dlg @3 resident.business [business=failing] : Poorly. I've dusted the same row three times this week. | Quiet. Wine keeps; I'm hoping the buyers do too.
dlg @1 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare] : #greet#. Prices are on the slate. Read them and then we'll talk. | #greet#. Good red, fair white, and no bargaining.
dlg @2 shop.greet [here.shop!=bare topic.meeting=known] : #topic.player#. There are two reds worth a look today. | You again. Good. You know how I do things.
dlg @3 shop.greet [here.shop=bare] : Racks near empty. Bring good stock and I'll buy it. | Thin racks until the carts arrive. Selling?
dlg @1 shop.prices : It's on the slate. It's the same on the slate as it is in my mouth. | Chalk's chalk. Read it and choose.
dlg @1 shop.bye : Carry it level. #farewell#. | Store it lying down. Off you go.
dlg @1 master.learn [here.teaches=yes] : You want the trade? Corks first. Then wax. Then you can look at the wine. | I'll teach anybody who can count and keep their temper. Better than I do.
dlg @2 master.learn [here.teaches=no] : I've a learner already. Ask me a question, though. I'll answer it straight.
dlg @1 master.takes [here.take_on=yes] : A single learner, and only someone I'd trust with a lamp in the cellar. You'd do. | Ask, and if I say yes, I mean it.
dlg @2 master.takes [here.take_on=no] : Not now. #here.take_on_why# I'll not pretend otherwise.
dlg @1 master.wages : No wage for learning. At my elbow you'll learn faster than alone, and that's better than coin.
dlg @1 master.bye : Back to the counter for me. #farewell#. | Go on. Practise the stroke.
graft resident.hub
choice Why do you keep that little blade on your belt? -> blade
node blade
say For #wax#. One stroke and the neck's clean. Twenty passes on #stone# each day before I unlock.
mood calm
free
choice Show me the stroke. -> blade_stroke
choice Back to the rest. -> hub
node blade_stroke
say Wrist, not the blade. You go under the wax and round, and it comes off in one curl. Two strokes and you've chipped the glass.
choice I'll try it some day. -> hub
choice Goodbye. -> bye

graft shop.greet
choice Will you take a little less for it? -> haggle
node haggle
say No. What's chalked is chalked, for you and for the Rise alike. I'll not start that dance.
mood stern
free
choice Then I'll pay what's chalked. -> haggle_paid
choice Back to the counter. -> greet
node haggle_paid
say Good. Then we're square, and you'll always get the better bottle from me.
mood pleased
choice Let me look at the stock. -> greet
choice Goodbye. -> bye
